    thisWeek: "Make a personal plan: Sunday Mass, a simple daily prayer, the next opportunity for Confession and one regular way to serve. For the adult: This final conversation is not an examination. Invite an honest conversation with the candidate and family about faith, practical arrangements and continuing accompaniment. Scripture and Catechism: Acts 2:42; Galatians 5:22–25; CCC 1306–1311 Appendix A | The Rite of Confirmation Step by Step Confirmation is normally celebrated within Mass. The bishop, or another minister lawfully authorised to confirm, and the approved liturgical book govern the exact celebration. These steps help candidates understand what they will do; the parish will also explain any practical arrangements. 1. Presentation and homily Candidates may be presented to the bishop. The minister explains how Confirmation strengthens Baptism and calls us to witness. 2. Renewal of baptismal promises Candidates renounce Satan and sin and profess the Catholic faith in the Father, the Son, the Holy Spirit and the Church. Answer sincerely with the congregation. 3. Prayer and laying on of hands The minister invites silent prayer, extends his hands over the candidates and asks the Father to send the sevenfold gift of the Holy Spirit. 4. Anointing with sacred chrism Approach reverently with your sponsor, according to parish guidance. The minister places his hand on your forehead in anointing you with chrism. 5. The sacramental words The minister says: “N., be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.” Answer clearly: “Amen.” 6. Sign of peace The minister says: “Peace be with you.” Answer: “And with your spirit.” 7. Continuation of Mass Join the prayers and Holy Eucharist. Receive Holy Communion if properly disposed, and afterwards give thanks to God for the gift of the Spirit. A helpful reminder The sacrament does not depend on giving a perfect answer, remembering an exact gesture or experiencing a dramatic feeling. The parish will guide you. Traditional descriptions sometimes mention a light blow on the cheek. The present Roman Rite uses the sign of peace after the anointing. Appendix B | The Seven Gifts and Twelve Fruits The gifts help us follow the promptings of the Holy Spirit. The fruits are qualities that grow in a life strengthened by his grace. They are not simply feelings and should be understood in the light of Catholic faith. Wisdom Loving and judging everything in the light of God and our final destiny. Understanding Grasping the truths of the faith more deeply. Counsel Recognising the right action in difficult or uncertain situations. Fortitude Standing firm and enduring difficulty for what is right. Knowledge Understanding creation and human life in relation to God. Piety Loving and worshipping God as our Father, with devotion and reverence. Fear of the Lord Reverent awe before God and loving sorrow at the thought of offending him. The twelve traditional fruits of the Holy Spirit Charity — Love of God above everything and our neighbour for God’s sake. Joy — Spiritual gladness rooted in God’s goodness. Peace — A heart increasingly ordered towards God and neighbour. Patience — Enduring difficulty without losing charity. Kindness — Being attentive and ready to do good. Goodness — Choosing what is right and generous. Generosity — Giving of ourselves without counting the cost. Gentleness — Strength used with humility and care. Faithfulness — Loyalty to God and reliability towards others. Modesty — Respectful restraint in dress, words and behaviour. Self-control — Governing desires and choices according to reason and grace. Chastity — Living sexuality with integrity according to our state of life and vocation. The gifts, fruits, virtues and charisms are related but not identical. See Conversation 9. Appendix C | Choosing a Sponsor and a Patron Saint What is a sponsor? A sponsor accompanies the candidate, presents the candidate for Confirmation and afterwards helps the newly confirmed person persevere as a witness of Christ. It is desirable, when suitable, to choose one of your baptismal godparents, showing the unity of Baptism and Confirmation. Who may ordinarily be a sponsor? • A practising Catholic who has received Confirmation and the Holy Eucharist. • A person ordinarily at least sixteen years old, unless a lawful exception applies. • Someone living in a way consistent with the Catholic faith and genuinely willing to fulfil the role. • Someone not prohibited from the role by a canonical penalty. • Someone other than the candidate’s father or mother. The parish priest will help if you are unsure whom to choose. Do not choose someone merely because the person is popular, related to you or willing to appear in a photograph. Do I need a Confirmation name? A new Confirmation name is not required. Where local custom allows it, some candidates choose the name of a saint. The important thing is not a fashionable or unusual name but a holy person whose example leads you closer to Christ. Your baptismal name can also be a good choice. Read the life of a saint, discover how that person followed Christ, and ask for their prayers. A sponsor and patron saint both point beyond themselves towards Jesus. My sponsor: _________________________________________________ Why this person will help me follow Christ: _____________________ My patron saint (if chosen): __________________________________ One virtue I would like to imitate: ___________________________ Appendix D | Prayers and a Catholic Rule of Life The Sign of the Cross In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. Amen. Come, Holy Spirit Come, Holy Spirit, fill the hearts of your faithful and kindle in them the fire of your love. Send forth your Spirit and they shall be created, and you shall renew the face of the earth. Amen. Glory Be Glory be to the Father, and to the Son, and to the Holy Spirit, as it was in the beginning, is now, and ever shall be, world without end. Amen. Our Father Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name; thy kingdom come; thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread; and forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us; and lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil. Amen. Hail Mary Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with thee; blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb, Jesus. Holy Mary, Mother of God, pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death. Amen. Act of Contrition — the parish form O my God, because you are so good, I am very sorry that I have sinned against you, and by the help of your grace I will not sin again. Amen. Prayer for the seven gifts Holy Spirit, give me wisdom to seek God, understanding to know the faith, counsel to choose rightly, fortitude to persevere, knowledge to see creation in God’s light, piety to love the Father and fear of the Lord to worship him with reverence. Amen. Before Confirmation Lord Jesus, I belong to you through Baptism. Prepare me to receive the seal of your Holy Spirit in the state of grace, with sincere faith, humility and joy. Amen. After Confirmation Father, I thank you for the Gift of the Holy Spirit. Keep me united to Jesus and his Church. Make my thoughts, words and actions a faithful witness to your love. Amen. A simple rule of Catholic life Every day Offer the day to God; pray to the Holy Spirit; read a short passage of Scripture; examine your conscience at night. Every Sunday Participate in the Holy Sacrifice of the Mass with faith and attention. Regularly Go to Confession, receive Holy Communion worthily, and make time for Adoration or quiet prayer. In relationships Practise honesty, kindness, chastity, forgiveness and care for anyone who is excluded or vulnerable. In the parish Discover a suitable way to serve within the parish’s safeguarding arrangements. When you fail Repent, seek God’s mercy and begin again. The Holy Spirit does not stop calling you to holiness. Appendix E | Summary Catechism These concise answers gather the principal teaching of the twelve conversations. First understand the meaning, then learn the answer. This is an optional aid to remembering, not a pass-or-fail examination. 1. Who is the Holy Spirit? The third Person of the Most Holy Trinity, true God with the Father and the Son. 2. Is the Holy Spirit merely a feeling? No. He is a divine Person who knows, loves, guides and sanctifies. 3. What happened at Pentecost? The Apostles received the Holy Spirit and proclaimed Christ courageously. 4. Which are the sacraments of Christian initiation? Baptism, Confirmation and the Holy Eucharist. 5. Does Confirmation replace Baptism? No. It strengthens and completes baptismal grace. 6. Who instituted Confirmation? Jesus Christ instituted Confirmation. 7. Where does Scripture show the Apostles giving the Spirit to the baptised? In Acts 8:14–17 and Acts 19:5–6. 8. Who is the ordinary minister of Confirmation in the Latin Church? The bishop. 9. Can a priest confirm? Yes, when Church law or competent authority gives him the faculty. 10. What is sacred chrism? Perfumed oil consecrated by a bishop. 11. What are the sacramental words in the Roman Rite? “N., be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.” 12. What does the candidate answer? “Amen.” 13. What is the spiritual seal of Confirmation? A permanent character marking us as belonging to Christ. 14. Can Confirmation be repeated? No. It is received only once. 15. What is the chief effect of Confirmation? A special outpouring of the Holy Spirit for stronger Christian life and witness. 16. How does Confirmation unite us to the Church? It binds us more perfectly to the Church and her mission. 17. What are the seven gifts? Wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety and fear of the Lord. 18. What do the gifts help us do? Follow the promptings of the Holy Spirit. 19. What are the theological virtues? Faith, hope and charity. 20. What are the cardinal virtues? Prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance. 21. What are the fruits of the Holy Spirit? Qualities of holiness formed in us by the Holy Spirit as we cooperate with grace. 22. What is a charism? A gift of grace for building up the Church and serving others. 23. Why renew baptismal promises? To profess again the faith received at Baptism and reject sin. 24. What does a soldier of Christ fight against? Sin, temptation, the fallen world and the devil, using spiritual means. 25. Does Christian witness allow hatred of opponents? No. We defend truth while loving every person. 26. Who may receive Confirmation? A baptised person who has not already been confirmed. 27. How should I prepare? Through prayer, faith, suitable instruction, sincere intention and proper disposition. 28. What state should I be in when confirmed? The state of grace. 29. Which sacrament should I receive beforehand when needed? The Sacrament of Penance and Reconciliation. 30. What does a sponsor do? Helps the confirmed person continue living as a witness of Christ. 31. Does Confirmation end my formation? No. I continue growing in faith and holiness throughout life. Appendix F | Readiness and Pastoral Conversation Readiness is not perfect memory, polished public speaking, attendance at a fixed number of meetings or reaching a particular age as a social milestone. A suitable candidate has a sincere intention to receive Confirmation, understands its essential meaning according to capacity, is properly disposed, and is willing to live as a disciple of Christ. Questions to explore with the candidate and family Faith in Christ Can the young person speak simply about Jesus Christ and profess the Catholic faith? Understanding of Confirmation Does the candidate understand the Holy Spirit, the relation to Baptism, the sacred anointing and the mission of witness? Free intention Does the candidate sincerely wish to receive the sacrament rather than feeling compelled merely to satisfy an adult or complete a programme? State of grace and Confession Has there been age-appropriate teaching about sin and a private opportunity for the Sacrament of Penance? Sunday Mass and prayer Is the candidate being helped to participate in the ordinary worship and prayer of the Church? Sponsor and practical arrangements Is there a suitable sponsor and a clear plan for the rite and necessary adjustments? Life afterwards What practical habits will help the candidate continue in Mass, prayer, Communion, Confession, charity and parish belonging? A few questions the priest or mentor may ask • Who is the Holy Spirit, and why does Confirmation matter to you? • What is the difference between Confirmation and finishing a course? • How might the Holy Spirit help you follow Jesus at home or among friends? • What would you like to ask about the faith or the sacrament? • What will help you continue practising your Catholic faith afterwards? Pastoral safety: Never require disclosure of personal sins or publicly shame a candidate. Respect disability, learning differences, anxiety and family circumstances. Arrange discreet pastoral and accessibility support and follow current Diocese of Nottingham safeguarding procedures. The parish priest makes the pastoral judgement about readiness within Church law and diocesan requirements. Confirmation is a gift of Christ. The purpose of preparation is a sincere, fruitful reception and a continuing Christian life. Sources and Doctrinal References Sacred Scripture Isaiah 11:1–3; John 14–16; Acts 1–2, 8:14–17, 19:5–6; Romans 8; 1 Corinthians 12–13; Galatians 5:22–25; Ephesians 6:10– 18; 1 Peter 3:15–16. Catechism of the Catholic Church Numbers 683–747 on the Holy Spirit; 1210–1321 on Christian initiation and Confirmation; 1691–1698 on life in Christ; 1803–1845 on virtues, gifts and fruits; 2006–2011 on grace and merit. Compendium of the Catechism of the Catholic Church The Holy Spirit and Church; questions on Confirmation and the Christian moral life. Code of Canon Law Canons 842, 879–896, 872–874, especially ministers, candidates, preparation, sponsors and sacramental records. The Order of Confirmation The approved Roman Rite for England and Wales governs the renewal of baptismal promises, invocation of the Spirit, anointing, words and sign of peace. Traditional Catholic catechesis Traditional descriptions of the confirmed as soldiers and witnesses of Christ, understood as spiritual combat against sin with charity for others. Diocese of Nottingham Current diocesan policy and safeguarding arrangements govern local preparation, age and particular cases. This catechism is intended as a faithful preparation resource. The approved liturgical books, universal Church law, current diocesan directions and the parish priest govern sacramental practice. It is not itself an authorisation to vary the rite or conditions for reception."
